\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces The bath spectrum predicts the cold spin before numerical comparison.~\cite  {McCaul2026e37796} (a) Lines show $-\langle \sigma _z\rangle _0=S_g+\Delta A_g$ and points show independent diagonalisation of the original Hamiltonian, with $\sigma _z=-\tau _x$. (b) The normalised conditional-bath trial predicts the dimensionless thermal coordinate $a'_0=-h_0$, compared with the coordinate obtained from those numerical ground states. Here $h_0=-\operatorname  {arctanh}r_0$, where $r_0$ is the reduced spin's Bloch-vector length. The two bath energies are $\nu _k=(0.2,1.8)$ and $v_k^2=4\nu _k e^{-\nu _k/2}$ in the chosen energy unit. The legend gives the tunnelling energy $\Delta $ and the horizontal axis the coupling $g$. Bath coupling is retained exactly, while tunnelling is controlled relative to the fixed bath gap. The comparison includes two oscillator cutoffs and a separate joint-vector check.}}{12}{figure.caption.5}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:dd-hmf-physical-ground-state-20261016}{{5}{12}{The bath spectrum predicts the cold spin before numerical comparison.~\cite {McCaul2026e37796} (a) Lines show $-\langle \sigma _z\rangle _0=S_g+\Delta A_g$ and points show independent diagonalisation of the original Hamiltonian, with $\sigma _z=-\tau _x$. (b) The normalised conditional-bath trial predicts the dimensionless thermal coordinate $a'_0=-h_0$, compared with the coordinate obtained from those numerical ground states. Here $h_0=-\operatorname {arctanh}r_0$, where $r_0$ is the reduced spin's Bloch-vector length. The two bath energies are $\nu _k=(0.2,1.8)$ and $v_k^2=4\nu _k e^{-\nu _k/2}$ in the chosen energy unit. The legend gives the tunnelling energy $\Delta $ and the horizontal axis the coupling $g$. Bath coupling is retained exactly, while tunnelling is controlled relative to the fixed bath gap. The comparison includes two oscillator cutoffs and a separate joint-vector check}{figure.caption.5}{}}
