\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7}{\ignorespaces Hierarchy moves the stability boundary of the sampled feedback model. (a) At $h/\tau _r=1$, each curve separates stable dynamics below it from instability above it. The orange and green bands show additional parameter ranges stabilised by a second and third aggregation layer. The $\alpha =0.35$ slice crosses at $N_c^{(1)}=138.25$, $N_c^{(2)}=217.17$ and $N_c^{(3)}=341.13$. (b) At fixed $\alpha =0.35$, increasing the update interval also raises the critical population. In both panels $\beta =0.6$, $b=1.2$ and $s=0.4$. Curves use the iterated Gaussian expected-majority model of Phases of Hierarchy~\cite  {McCaul630942}, not exact small-block voting or ANF's different feedback rule. The numerical checks of the linear boundary are retained with the figure's result record.}}{17}{figure.caption.7}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:dd-hierarchy-stability}{{7}{17}{Hierarchy moves the stability boundary of the sampled feedback model. (a) At $h/\tau _r=1$, each curve separates stable dynamics below it from instability above it. The orange and green bands show additional parameter ranges stabilised by a second and third aggregation layer. The $\alpha =0.35$ slice crosses at $N_c^{(1)}=138.25$, $N_c^{(2)}=217.17$ and $N_c^{(3)}=341.13$. (b) At fixed $\alpha =0.35$, increasing the update interval also raises the critical population. In both panels $\beta =0.6$, $b=1.2$ and $s=0.4$. Curves use the iterated Gaussian expected-majority model of Phases of Hierarchy~\cite {McCaul630942}, not exact small-block voting or ANF's different feedback rule. The numerical checks of the linear boundary are retained with the figure's result record}{figure.caption.7}{}}
